WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Before a model can call any tool through MCP, you need a server to expose that tool.

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:11.279
The MCP Python SDK gives you everything you need to build one, from the transport layer

3
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.799
to the request routing. In this lesson, we'll walk through installation, project structure,

4
00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:21.920
server initialization, and the lifecycle hooks that keep everything running reliably.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.600
Don't worry about the complexity of this slide. We're going to dig into things.

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.799
I put all of this on the slide so that you can review it later if you're the slides only type.

7
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:38.080
Let's dig in. An MCP server is a process, not a library you import into your application.

8
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.759
It runs on its own, exposes tools, resources, and prompt templates to clients over a defined

9
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:49.520
transport, and handles capability registration, request dispatch, and response formatting.

10
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.439
That process boundary is exactly why the MCP inspector exists. The inspector is the official

11
00:00:55.439 --> 00:01:00.479
debugging tool, and it attaches to your server so you can see what is registered,

12
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:06.879
and fire test requests without building a client first. What you register are MCP tools,

13
00:01:06.879 --> 00:01:11.760
callable functions the model can invoke to perform an action or return a computed result.

14
00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:17.519
Tools are model controlled, meaning the AI decides when and how to call them,

15
00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:24.639
not your application code. Installing the MCP SDK is straightforward. A single pip install

16
00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:31.360
MCP command pulls in the server runtime, transport adapters, and type stubs. Add it to a requirements

17
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:38.160
file so your server is reproducible across environments. Python 3.10 or higher is required

18
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.000
because the SDK uses async features introduced in that version. Working inside a virtual environment

19
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.080
keeps your MCP dependencies isolated from other projects on the same machine. That separation

20
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.959
matters when you're running multiple servers locally during development, each potentially

21
00:01:54.959 --> 00:02:00.800
needing different versions of the same underlying libraries. Starting clean saves debugging time

22
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.959
later when dependency conflicts would otherwise be difficult to trace. The transport layer

23
00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:13.039
determines how clients and servers communicate. The stdio transport is the simplest option.

24
00:02:13.039 --> 00:02:19.839
The client spawns the server as a subprocess, writes JSON RPC messages to its stand-in,

25
00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:25.119
and reads responses from its standout. It's perfect for local development and tools like

26
00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:31.919
Cloud Desktop that manage server processes directly. The streamable HTTP transport runs

27
00:02:31.919 --> 00:02:39.679
the server as a standalone process that listens on a URL clients connect over HTTP, and the server

28
00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:46.639
can optionally use server sent events to stream messages back. Choose streamable HTTP when multiple

29
00:02:46.639 --> 00:02:53.360
clients need to connect simultaneously or when the server runs remotely. The older standalone HTTP

30
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.919
plus SSE transport is now deprecated. The SDK supports both transports with the same tool

31
00:02:59.919 --> 00:03:06.800
registration API. Every MCP server follows the same three-part structure. You create an instance

32
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:13.119
of the MCP server class and give it a name. You register tools, resources, and prompts on that

33
00:03:13.119 --> 00:03:18.800
instance using decorators. Then you call run with your transport and the server blocks, processing

34
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:25.119
requests until it shuts down or crashes. Run wraps a syncio under the hood, so you never manage the

35
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:32.399
event loop yourself. Version 2 of the SDK renamed this class from FastMCP, and the old import path

36
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:37.919
is gone, so upgrading breaks any project that still reaches for the old name. There is also a

37
00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:44.320
lower level server class, but it takes its handlers as constructor arguments instead of decorators,

38
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.119
so the two aren't interchangeable. Everything ahead builds on the MCP server class.

39
00:03:50.399 --> 00:03:56.639
A recommended project layout separates concerns into four files. The server.py entry point

40
00:03:56.639 --> 00:04:04.240
creates the MCP server instance and calls run. A tools.py module holds all tool definitions

41
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:11.440
and handler functions imported at startup. A requirements dot txt pins MCP and any tool

42
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.519
dependencies, so deployments are reproducible across machines and environments. A readme documents

43
00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:23.760
the transport, available tools, and any environment variables the server requires to start. This

44
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.040
structure keeps the entry point readable and makes it easy to add new tools without touching

45
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.760
the startup logic. As your server grows, you can split tools.py into domain-specific modules

46
00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.720
without changing how the server itself is initialized or started. The MCP server class

47
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.359
manages startup and shutdown through a single lifespan async context manager, not separate hooks.

48
00:04:49.359 --> 00:04:55.600
You write an async function decorated with async context manager and pass it to the constructor.

49
00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.320
Everything before the yield statement runs once at startup, so that's where you open database

50
00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.880
connections, load configuration, and warm any caches your tools need. The yield hands control

51
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.040
to the running server. When the server shuts down, execution resumes after the yield, and a finally

52
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.200
block is where you close connections, flush buffers, and release file handles cleanly. Because it's

53
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.880
async, you can await inside it. Your handlers reach those shared resources through the lifespan

54
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.239
context, so setup and teardown live in one clear place instead of scattered callbacks.

55
00:05:31.519 --> 00:05:36.959
When a client first connects, the server and client exchange capability lists. The server

56
00:05:36.959 --> 00:05:43.440
declares which primitives it supports, tools, resources, or prompts. The client inspects the list

57
00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.880
and decides which ones it will use. It won't appear in the capability list, and the client won't be

58
00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.920
able to call it. This handshake is also where the server announces its name and version, which

59
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.440
appears in client logs and debugging tools. Understanding the negotiation sequence helps you

60
00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.239
diagnose connection issues during development, because a missing tool is almost always a

61
00:06:04.239 --> 00:06:10.079
registration ordering problem rather than a deeper protocol issue. Three initialization

62
00:06:10.079 --> 00:06:16.239
mistakes come up repeatedly. The first is registering tools after run is called. The

63
00:06:16.239 --> 00:06:22.640
capability handshake happens at connection time, so any tool you add afterward is invisible to the

64
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.040
client. The second is blocking the event loop inside a tool handler with synchronous I-O, like a

65
00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:36.160
file read or a database query, using a sync library. One blocking call stalls every other request the

66
00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:44.399
server is processing. Use async libraries or wrap sync calls with run in executor to avoid this.

67
00:06:44.959 --> 00:06:51.519
The third is leaving tool handlers without explicit error handling. The MCP server class

68
00:06:51.519 --> 00:06:58.239
does catch unhandled exceptions and surface them as error results, so the server keeps running,

69
00:06:58.239 --> 00:07:04.320
but you should still handle errors yourself, raising tool error or masking details to return

70
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:10.959
clean, actionable messages and avoid leaking internals to the client. Standing up a server

71
00:07:10.959 --> 00:07:17.119
and writing a tool are different jobs, and the skeleton is the one that people rush. Startup work

72
00:07:17.119 --> 00:07:23.200
belongs in the Lifespan Context Manager, where connections open once and close cleanly.

73
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:29.679
Everything else is per request. Anything registered after run is already invisible,

74
00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:33.440
because the handshake settled capabilities before your code even got there.

75
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.959
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next video.

